%% The deployer Role's boundary — Multi-Tenancy & Workload Security.
%% The step-3 picture on the Demo page: the CI identity ships a Deployment and is refused on
%% Secrets, with the allowed path and the denied one side by side. The point is that the denial
%% needs no deny rule — RBAC is deny-by-default, so a verb the Role never lists simply has nothing
%% to match. That is what `edit` would have given away, and the reason a custom Role is written.
%% ---
%% NEVER put an angle-bracket placeholder in a .mmd file. Antora emits mermaid source into the page
%% UNESCAPED, so the browser parses it as a real unknown element, which never closes and swallows
%% the rest of the graph — the diagram renders as "Syntax error in text".
%% ---
%% NO SUBGRAPHS ON PURPOSE, and the two outcomes are deliberately NOT wrapped as one: their colours
%% are the whole lesson. A `direction` line would be discarded here anyway, because every edge would
%% cross the boundary (diagram-size-guard rule 1). Longest chain is three nodes.
flowchart LR
  sa["ServiceAccount<br/>payments-ops"] --> role["deployer Role<br/>deployments · pods/log"]
  role -->|"a rule matches"| ok["oc rollout restart<br/>Deployment updated"]
  role -->|"no rule matches"| deny["oc get secrets<br/>Forbidden"]
  classDef id fill:#e8eaf6,stroke:#3949ab,color:#1a237e;
  classDef rb fill:#e00,stroke:#900,color:#fff;
  classDef good fill:#e6f4ea,stroke:#137333,color:#0d652d;
  classDef bad fill:#fce8e6,stroke:#c5221f,color:#a50e0e;
  class sa id;
  class role rb;
  class ok good;
  class deny bad;
